bullish than polls alone imply", "Open seat in an even-PVI state with a Democratic-tilting national environment"], "rationale": "Mid-August likely-voter polls uniformly put Crowley up three to five points, most race raters call it Lean D, and markets price him near 80-85 percent; Tiffany's large cash advantage and the month-old polling keep uncertainty wide, so this stays Lean D rather than Likely D.", "watch": "The September 30 Marquette poll will be the first fresh reading since August; if Tiffany's spending closes the gap, the race moves back toward a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Tony Evers (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Wisconsin's governorship is open for the first time in years, with Tony Evers retiring and Milwaukee County executive David Crowley carrying the Democratic banner against Republican congressman Tom Tiffany. The state's even partisan balance makes this a natural battleground, and the contest has centered on affordability, energy, and data centers.\n\nThe most recent polls, from mid-August, all showed Crowley ahead by three to five points among likely voters, and most race raters call the contest Lean D, with two calling it a Tossup. Tiffany holds a large money advantage, having raised roughly ten times as much as Crowley, which keeps the race within reach. Prediction markets are more bullish on Crowley than the polls, pricing his chances around 80 to 85 percent. Late-September headlines have highlighted Tiffany's press-access disputes and a skipped debate, while both campaigns press affordability themes into the final stretch.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7127, "margin": 2.83}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7543, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7549, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7725, "margin": 3.8}]}